  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Navas T vs The Tahsildar (Lr) -Thalassery on 24 June, 2025 2025:Ker:45569 Wp(C) No.2874 Of 2025 1 In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present The Honourable Mr.Justice Mohammed Nias C.P. Tuesday, The 24Th Day Of June 2025 / 3Rd Ashadha, 1947",
    "facts": "The counter affidavit on behalf of the government states that the petitioner had encroached upon Government land, and on noticing the same, he was given a notice on 21.12.2015 requiring him to vacate the land. The petitioner then filed O.S No.06 of 2016 on the files of the Munsiff Court, Thalassery and obtained an interim injunction restraining the removal of the petty shop otherwise than by the due process of law. In accordance with the said order, the village Officer Thalassery submitted A Form report under the provisions of the Kerala Land Conservancy Act, and thereafter, the Tahsildar (LR) issued a B Form notice on 23.07.2016. The petitioner did not participate in the enquiry. 4. A second notice was issued on 10.08.2016. After a due enquiry, the Tahsildar had passed an order on 30.07.2019 directing the petitioner to vacate the land within 15 days. On receipt of the same, the petitioner filed WP(C) No.22480 of 2019 before this Court, which was disposed of on 02.12.2019, directing the petitioner to invoke the statutory remedies. Thereafter, the petitioner filed an application before the Sub Collector, Thalassery, as per the provisions of the Street Vendors Act instead of filing any appeal against the order of the Tahsildar (LR) referred to above, under the provisions of the Kerala Land Conservancy Act and Rules, which has become final. 2025:KER:45569 WP(C) NO.2874 OF 2025 5. The petitioner thereafter filed WP(C) No.27543 of 2020, which was disposed of, recording the submission of the Standing Counsel for the Thalassery Municipality that a street vending licence was granted to the petitioner. 6. The petitioner had also filed case No.24 of 2020 before the Grievance Committee constituted under Section 20 of the Act for redressal of the grievance of street vendors. Final order was passed on the same on 29 th April 2021, which reads as follows; Navas T vs The Tahsildar (Lr) -Thalassery on 24 June, 2025 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/169201334/ 3 \"In the result, we grant injunction against the respondents from preventing the petitioner to do vending of Fast Food, Tea etc in the mobile cart which vehicle he can keep lay the side of the public road and that too after complying with Kovid-19 Protocols. But this order is not giving permission to the petitioner to use any portions of the puramboke land for his vending. The Revenue authorities can prevent him from encroaching on the puramboke land for vending except for keeping his mobile vending cart from which the petitioner can vend. The Revenue and Municipal authorities is directed to obey this order in view of the permissions in the Central Act.No.07/2014.\" 7. The above clearly shows that the petitioner could not have encroached upon the Government land, and the petitioner and the Municipality were bound by the final order dated 29 th April 2021. Again, a notice was issued to the petitioner to vacate the encroachment, and the entire area was vacated, and the petitioner was removed on 13.01.2025 from the Government land. 8. The prayer is as such in the writ petition became infructuous. However, as contended by the learned counsel for the petitioner that he still holds a licence under the Street Vendors (Protection of Livelihood and Regulation of Street Vending) Act, 2014, as seen from Ext.P6 dated 25.09.2024, which is valid till 24.09.2026, it will be open to the petitioner to request respondent No.4 to specify the place wherein he can carry on the business, for which Ext.P6 licence has been granted.",
